# `operators::gradient`

The vector of first partial derivatives of a scalar function.

## Signature

```cpp
operators::Operator operators::gradient(pluss::StackArray<std::string_view> variables = {}, operators::DiffOptions options = {});
```

## What It Does

`gradient()` is the operator `f ↦ ∇f = (∂f/∂x₁, …, ∂f/∂xₙ)`. It takes a scalar-valued function of `n` variables and returns a vector-valued function of the same variables whose single output axis has length `n`. With no arguments the partials are taken with respect to every variable of the function, in declaration order; naming variables, `gradient({"z", "x"})`, takes only those, in the order given.

The gradient points in the direction of steepest increase and is the object of gradient descent, of the first-order optimality condition `∇f = 0`, and of the normal to a level set. It evaluates on arrays of points: for a batch of `m` points the result has shape `[m, n]`.

The input must be scalar-valued (rank 0); for a vector-valued function use [`jacobian`](jacobian.md). Mixed and higher derivatives are in [`hessian`](hessian.md).

## Parameters

`pluss::StackArray<std::string_view> variables`
: The variables to differentiate with respect to, in the order they should appear in the result. Empty (the default) means all variables of the function, in declaration order.

`operators::DiffOptions options`
: Non-smooth policy, fallback and size limit.

## Returns

An `Operator` whose application returns the gradient as a rank-1 `Function` of length equal to the number of variables.

## Errors

- The input must be scalar-valued: `input Function must be scalar-valued`.
- An unknown variable raises `unknown variable '<name>'`.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

The gradient of a function of three variables at a point, with all variables (declaration order) and with a chosen subset in a chosen order. For `f = xy + yz²` at `(1, 2, 3)`: `∇f = (y, x + z², 2yz) = (2, 10, 12)`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const math::Function f = math::function({"x", "y", "z"}, [](math::Function x, math::Function y, math::Function z) { return x * y + y * z * z; });
  const auto point = {pluss::make_series(1.0), pluss::make_series(2.0), pluss::make_series(3.0)};
  const math::Function all = operators::gradient()(f);
  const math::Function some = operators::gradient({"z", "x"})(f);
  out.writeln("all variables:  shape", all.shape(), all(point).items<double>());
  out.writeln("{z, x}:         shape", some.shape(), some(point).items<double>(), " (f_z = 12, f_x = 2)");
}
```

### Example 2

A batch of points gives one gradient per point: shape `[points, variables]`. Three points of `f = xy + yz²`, the same value in each variable.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const math::Function f = math::function({"x", "y", "z"}, [](math::Function x, math::Function y, math::Function z) { return x * y + y * z * z; });
  const auto t = pluss::make_series({1.0, 2.0, 3.0});
  const pluss::Series g = operators::gradient()(f)({t, t, t});
  out.writeln("shape:", g.sizes());
  out.writeln("gradients:", g.reshape({9}).items<double>(), " (y, x + z^2, 2yz at t, t, t)");
}
```

### Example 3

Gradient descent. A loop that follows the negative gradient of the Rosenbrock-type bowl `(x − 1)² + 10 (y − x²)²` converges toward its minimum at `(1, 1)`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(5));
  const math::Function f = math::function({"x", "y"}, [](math::Function x, math::Function y) { return (x - 1.0) * (x - 1.0) + 10.0 * (y - x * x) * (y - x * x); });
  const math::Function g = operators::gradient()(f);
  double x = -0.5, y = 0.5;
  for (int step = 0; step <= 400; ++step) {
    const auto gradient = g({pluss::make_series(x), pluss::make_series(y)}).items<double>();
    if (step % 100 == 0) out.writeln("step", step, ": (x, y) =", x, y, "  f =", f({pluss::make_series(x), pluss::make_series(y)}).item<double>());
    x -= 0.01 * gradient[0];
    y -= 0.01 * gradient[1];
  }
}
```

### Example 4

The input must be scalar-valued, and the variables must exist.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <string>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const auto first_line = [](const pluss::Error &e) {
    const std::string text(e.what());
    return text.substr(0, text.find('\n'));
  };

  const math::Function vector = math::function({"x", "y"}, [](math::Function x, math::Function y) { return math::stack({x, y}); });
  const math::Function scalar = math::function({"x", "y"}, [](math::Function x, math::Function y) { return x * y; });
  try { (void)operators::gradient()(vector); } catch (const pluss::Error &e) { out.writeln("vector-valued input:", first_line(e)); }
  try { (void)operators::gradient({"w"})(scalar); } catch (const pluss::Error &e) { out.writeln("unknown variable:   ", first_line(e)); }
}
```

